diff --git a/.garura/project/issues/882/context/understand/20261025T111751Z/caps/cap-initialize/sources/issue-862.md b/.garura/project/issues/882/context/understand/20261025T111751Z/caps/cap-initialize/sources/issue-862.md
--- /dev/null
+++ b/.garura/project/issues/882/context/understand/20261025T111751Z/caps/cap-initialize/sources/issue-862.md
@@ -0,0 +1,24 @@
+# #862 A project gets only the memory and the review rules that fit it: initialize asks after the install
+
+**Hypothesis:** We believe a project needs only the part of Phoenix memory that fits it. Today an install copies all of the memory, and every review runs every rule, so a project that has no database, no front end or no permissions still gets those standards and those findings. We believe that when a project answers a short set of questions after the install, it gets the memory and the review rules that fit it, and nothing else.
+
+**We will know it is true when:**
+- after the install, one play (`initialize`) asks the person a short set of questions built from Phoenix memory: the domains, the kinds of features, the technology standards, the tools and frameworks, the surfaces (web, mobile, CLI, API), and whether there is a database, personal data, permissions or a running service;
+- each question has choices and a proposed answer with its source;
+- the answers set up the project's Garura memory from only the part of Phoenix memory it needs, and the profile records them;
+- a review in that project runs only the rule groups that fit it (for Garura itself: no front end, no back-end service, no database, no running service), and the review record names the groups it skipped;
+- a project can run `initialize` again when it changes.
+
+**Rules**
+1. The install brings no memory of its own; `initialize` builds the project's memory after it (recorded with the #653 plan, ADR 038, ADR 039).
+2. Every part reads memory only through the memory tool.
+3. The answers are the person's; a proposed answer always names its source.
+
+**Out of scope:**
+- Moving the memory tool's home to Phoenix, and removing `~/.garura/core/memory`: later, once the memory tool is fully live.
+- Installed parts reading the source tree (#860).
+
+**Identified by:** #860 (the review of PR #858 ran database, permission and front-end rules on Garura's own tool files).
+
+Recorded with the product owner, 2026-10-25.
+
diff --git a/.garura/project/issues/882/context/understand/20261025T111751Z/caps/cap-initialize/sources/issue-881.md b/.garura/project/issues/882/context/understand/20261025T111751Z/caps/cap-initialize/sources/issue-881.md
--- /dev/null
+++ b/.garura/project/issues/882/context/understand/20261025T111751Z/caps/cap-initialize/sources/issue-881.md
@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+# #881 Initialize sets up the tracker's work-item types: Business Intent, Epic, Story, Bug, Chore and Spike
+
+**When a project is initialized, its tracker gets the work-item types Garura files.** Garura files its work as issues of fixed types: Business Intent, Epic, Story, Bug, Chore and Spike. A tracker does not always have them. On 2026-10-25 Garura's own GitHub organization had no Epic type, so `/shape-plan` could not file an Epic as an Epic (#875). Each project hits the same gap the first time a play writes to its tracker.
+
+So `initialize` (this business intent) also sets up the tracker: whatever the project uses — GitHub, Jira, Linear or another tracker — it finds the work-item types the plays need, and makes the ones that are missing.
+
+## Done when
+
+- `initialize` reads which tracker the project uses, and the types that tracker has.
+- It lists the types the plays need (Business Intent, Epic, Story, Bug, Chore, Spike), each with its one-line meaning, and says which are missing.
+- It makes each missing type only after the person's typed yes, and records what it made.
+- Where a tracker cannot make a type, it says so in plain words and names the type it uses in its place; the plays read that mapping.
+- GitHub works first. Jira and Linear come through the same adapter, each with its own way to make a type.
+- A play that writes to the tracker stops with a plain message when a type it needs is missing, and points to `initialize`.
+
+## Context
+
+- Found in plan 788, while fixing #875: the organization `intent-driven-ai` got the Epic type by hand on 2026-10-25.
+- Not planned yet. It is worked when this business intent's plan starts.
diff --git a/.garura/project/issues/882/context/understand/20261025T111751Z/caps/cap-initialize/sources/issue-883.md b/.garura/project/issues/882/context/understand/20261025T111751Z/caps/cap-initialize/sources/issue-883.md
--- /dev/null
+++ b/.garura/project/issues/882/context/understand/20261025T111751Z/caps/cap-initialize/sources/issue-883.md
@@ -0,0 +1,32 @@
+# #883 All memory in Garura's source is Phoenix memory: one install lays it, initialize gives each project its own blocks
+
+**All memory in Garura's source is Phoenix memory, and it reaches projects in three steps** (the product owner, 2026-10-25):
+
+1. **The Phoenix install, once per machine.** It lays Phoenix memory from the `phoenix-memory` repo at `~/.phoenix`. The memory tool reads Phoenix memory only through its own interface. Today that interface points at `~/.phoenix`; later it points at a remote place, and nothing else changes.
+2. **The Garura install, once per project.** It lays only the pieces: the plays, agents, skills, tools and the memory tool. It lays no memory. "Garura cannot install its memory."
+3. **`initialize`, after the Garura install.** It asks the questions that decide which memory fits the project (for example Java or .NET, a React front end or none) and sets up the project's Garura memory from Phoenix memory. It can run again to bring changes in.
+
+A person installs Phoenix once on a machine and initializes many projects, each with different memory blocks.
+
+## Why
+
+Today the source memory and the `phoenix-memory` repo drift apart. They are kept in step by hand. On 2026-10-25 the feature page template was in the source but in no memory tier, and Phoenix memory still had the old `functionality.md` (#868). A play that wrote a feature page could not be judged until the template was promoted by hand.
+
+## Done when
+
+- Every memory file in Garura's source is classed as Phoenix memory.
+- The Phoenix install lays Phoenix memory from the `phoenix-memory` repo; a second Phoenix install brings it up to date.
+- The Garura install lays no memory of its own.
+- `initialize` builds the project's Garura memory from Phoenix memory, with only the blocks its answers call for, and can run again to take in changes.
+- Two projects on one machine, initialized with different answers, get different memory blocks from the one Phoenix memory.
+- A play reads memory only through the memory tool.
+
+## Open
+
+- How the source memory reaches the `phoenix-memory` repo without hand copying (#868).
+
+## Context
+
+- Under business intent #862 (initialize). Not planned yet.
+- ADR 038 already says the Garura install brings no memory. It says the memory tool downloads Phoenix memory on first use; this story replaces that with the Phoenix install.
+
