diff --git a/.garura/project/issues/882/context/understand/20261025T132647Z/judge/round3/verdict-fn-881.json b/.garura/project/issues/882/context/understand/20261025T132647Z/judge/round3/verdict-fn-881.json
--- /dev/null
+++ b/.garura/project/issues/882/context/understand/20261025T132647Z/judge/round3/verdict-fn-881.json
@@ -0,0 +1,37 @@
+{
+  "doc": ".garura/product/product-os/software-delivery-planning/initialize/fn-881/feature.md",
+  "kind": "feature",
+  "criteria": [
+    {
+      "id": "completeness",
+      "verdict": "pass",
+      "evidence": "\"It lists each needed type with its one-line meaning and says which are there, which are missing and which the tracker cannot make.\" \"Out: the mapping: each needed type and the tracker type it is filed as, its own or a stand-in the product owner said yes to.\" \"Given a GitHub project whose organization has no Epic type, when initialize runs, then the brief lists the six types with their meanings and marks Epic as missing.\" \"Asking the other setup questions (Ask the setup questions, fn-setup-questions, owns them).\"",
+      "fix": ""
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "self_explanation",
+      "verdict": "pass",
+      "evidence": "\"When every needed type is already there, initialize says so and makes nothing, because a second copy of a type would split the team's work items between two types.\" \"that type gets no stand-in and stays unmapped, because a person with the right can still make the real type.\" \"It goes on with the other approved types, because one failure says nothing about them.\"",
+      "fix": ""
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "discreteness_form",
+      "verdict": "pass",
+      "evidence": "\"Given a tracker whose adapter entry says it cannot make the Spike type, when initialize runs, then the brief says so in plain words and proposes a stand-in for Spike with why it is the closest.\" \"Given Epic and Spike are missing, when the product owner types yes for Epic only, then only Epic is made, and the brief and the record name Spike as still missing.\"",
+      "fix": ""
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "depth_gradient",
+      "verdict": "pass",
+      "evidence": "\"A tracker cannot make a type when the adapter says the tracker has no way to add a type of its own, for example a tracker with a fixed list of types; that type gets a proposed stand-in.\" \"Out: a record of what was made and what was mapped, kept beside the mapping in the project's settings, so a later run and a person both find it where the plays read the mapping: each type, made / already there / stand-in / failed / still missing, the date, and for a failure what failed.\"",
+      "fix": ""
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "stranger_test",
+      "verdict": "pass",
+      "evidence": "\"Words this page uses. The tracker is the system where the project's team keeps its work items (GitHub, Jira, Linear or another).\" \"It exists because a tracker does not always have these types: on 2026-10-25 Garura's own GitHub organization had no Epic type, so /shape-plan could not file an Epic as an Epic (#875).\" \"Filing epics, stories and other work items (/shape-plan and the other filing plays own them).\"",
+      "fix": ""
+    }
+  ],
+  "prior": []
+}
diff --git a/.garura/project/issues/882/context/understand/20261025T132647Z/judge/round3/verdict-fn-883.json b/.garura/project/issues/882/context/understand/20261025T132647Z/judge/round3/verdict-fn-883.json
--- /dev/null
+++ b/.garura/project/issues/882/context/understand/20261025T132647Z/judge/round3/verdict-fn-883.json
@@ -0,0 +1,37 @@
+{
+  "doc": ".garura/product/product-os/software-delivery-planning/initialize/fn-883/feature.md",
+  "kind": "feature",
+  "criteria": [
+    {
+      "id": "completeness",
+      "verdict": "pass",
+      "evidence": "\"It records, in the project, which blocks the project holds and which parts of Phoenix memory each was built from, as they were then, so the project can learn later that Phoenix memory changed.\" \"Out: when Phoenix memory cannot be reached, a plain message that says so and that the Phoenix install must run on this machine, and no memory built.\" \"Given a brief that names two changed parts in the database block, when the product owner types yes to the first and no to the second\" \"Laying the plays, agents, skills, tools and the memory tool in a project: the Garura install owns it, and it lays no memory.\"",
+      "fix": ""
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "self_explanation",
+      "verdict": "pass",
+      "evidence": "\"It never writes Phoenix memory and never writes another project's memory, because Phoenix memory is shared by every project on the machine and a write there from one project would change what the others draw from.\" \"The reply is per part because one changed standard can be welcome and another in the same block not, and a yes for the whole block only would force the product owner to take both.\" \"An empty answer there would look like \"no block fits\" and leave the project with too little memory.\"",
+      "fix": ""
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "discreteness_form",
+      "verdict": "pass",
+      "evidence": "\"Given a project that took the database block and Phoenix memory added a new part where the block's selection looks, when `initialize` next runs in the project, then its brief names the new part as added under the database block.\" \"A part the list holds whose fingerprint differs is changed.\"",
+      "fix": ""
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "depth_gradient",
+      "verdict": "pass",
+      "evidence": "\"For each block it holds the answer that selected it, the block's selection, for each part the block was built from its place in Phoenix memory (the file and the part of it) and its fingerprint (a short code that changes when that part's content changes), and the version of Phoenix memory the block was built from.\" \"A part the selection now finds that the list does not hold is added. A part the list holds that the selection no longer finds is taken out.\"",
+      "fix": ""
+    },
+    {
+      "id": "stranger_test",
+      "verdict": "pass",
+      "evidence": "\"Memory reaches a project in three steps, and this feature is the third.\" \"It keeps each project's blocks its own. A person installs Phoenix once on a machine and initializes many projects there, each with different answers, so two projects hold different blocks taken from the one Phoenix memory, and initializing one project never changes another project or Phoenix memory.\" \"Adding or taking out a whole block when an answer changes: Run initialize again (fn-rerun-initialize) owns it.\"",
+      "fix": ""
+    }
+  ],
+  "prior": []
+}
