+{
+  "doc": ".garura/product/product-os/software-delivery-planning/initialize/fn-project-memory/feature.md",
+  "kind": "feature",
+  "criteria": [
+    {"id": "completeness", "verdict": "pass", "evidence": "\"Runs after the product owner confirms the setup answers. It sets up the project's Garura memory from only the memory blocks those answers call for,\" \"Out: when Phoenix memory cannot be reached, a plain message that says so and that the Phoenix install must run on this machine, and no project memory built.\" \"Given a second run where the database answer changed from yes to no, when the memory is rebuilt, then the profile holds the new answer and the record names the database parts that were taken out.\" \"Keeping each project's memory blocks its own and finding, on a later run, that Phoenix memory changed under them (Give each project its own memory blocks, fn-883, owns it).\"", "fix": ""},
+    {"id": "self_explanation", "verdict": "pass", "evidence": "\"because a whole file brings context the project does not need (all of commerce for a project that needs only checkout), and the plays would read that context as if it fit the project.\" \"It goes on because one answer with no matching standard does not make the other answers wrong; this is unlike an unreachable Phoenix memory, where nothing read can be trusted.\" \"An empty result here would look like \"no file fits\" and leave the project with too little memory.\"", "fix": ""},
+    {"id": "discreteness_form", "verdict": "pass", "evidence": "\"An answer that names no place (for example database no) selects no block.\" \"A part that two selected blocks both need is built once and names both answers that selected it, so a later run that drops one of the two answers keeps it for the other.\" \"Given a checkout answer and a commerce knowledge file in Phoenix memory that also holds catalog, cart and orders, when the memory is built, then the project's Garura memory holds the checkout part and none of the rest of that file.\"", "fix": ""},
+    {"id": "depth_gradient", "verdict": "pass", "evidence": "\"How a block is found, and how the part is cut from a file, are our default: a part is cut along the file's own sections (a heading and what is under it), and a section is kept when it speaks to the answer;\" \"When an answer selects nothing in Phoenix memory (for example a tool memory has no standard for), the record says so in plain words, and the project's memory is still built from the other answers.\" \"The record says which blocks and parts were added and which were taken out,\"", "fix": ""},
+    {"id": "stranger_test", "verdict": "pass", "evidence": "\"The Garura install lays no memory; this feature is where the project's Garura memory is first written.\" \"Each answer selects its memory block, and only the part of Phoenix memory that belongs to the block of an answer the product owner gave is built into the project.\" \"Laying Phoenix memory on the machine and bringing it up to date: the Phoenix install owns it, once per machine. This feature only reads what is already laid.\"", "fix": ""}
+  ],
+  "prior": [
+    {"id": "discreteness_form", "fixed": true, "evidence": "\"How an answer is tied to its parts: each choice that Ask the setup questions (fn-setup-questions) offers is built from one named place in Phoenix memory:\" \"To build the block, initialize asks the memory tool for what the selection finds, reads it, and builds the project's memory from only the parts that speak to the answer, and from no others.\" \"An answer that names no place (for example database no) selects no block.\" \"A part that two selected blocks both need is built once and names both answers that selected it,\" \"Phoenix memory (a word this page uses) is the one shared memory on a machine\" \"A memory block (a word this page uses) is the relevant part of Phoenix memory that one setup answer selects,\""}
+  ]
+}
