++{"citation_id": "cite-20261025-018", "issue": 882, "play": "/discover-capabilities", "question": "Which knowledge shelf does the initialize capability (set a project up after the install: memory, review rules, tracker types) belong to?", "answer": "None of the ten domain shelves; it stays under the model domain software-delivery-planning, which carries its own outside sources.", "decided_by": "product-os-keeper", "decided_at": "2026-10-25T10:30:05Z", "trace": [{"asked": "a domain shelf whose trigger covers setting a project up for agentic software delivery", "found": "the closest shelf, user-management, triggers on accounts, login, profiles, roles or access control, which is not this work; no shelf triggers on software delivery", "source": {"tier": "phoenix", "path": "knowledge/domains/user-management.md", "start": 4, "end": 4, "fingerprint": "sha256:c8986449c7e26f568492b1aa250a9a0b8ef2604029dacde362a7b72a9dc4c529", "text": "conditions:", "version": "3161dcdfe26f", "around": {"start": 1, "end": 7, "text": "---\nid: domains/user-management\ntitle: \"User Management: capabilities and when to include each (profile-driven)\"\nconditions:\n  trigger: \"the product needs accounts, login, profiles, roles, or access control\"\n  selection_keys: [shape.users, shape.surfaces, nfr.security, nfr.accessibility, compliance]\nprovenance: seeded"}}}], "from_update": null}
++{"citation_id": "cite-20261025-019", "issue": 882, "play": "/discover-capabilities", "question": "What does feature fn-883 of cap-initialize do?", "answer": "Every memory file in the source is Phoenix memory; one install lays it on the machine and a second install brings it up to date; initialize gives each project only the blocks its answers call for, so two projects on one machine get different blocks.", "decided_by": "product-os-keeper", "decided_at": "2026-10-25T10:40:20Z", "trace": [{"asked": "what #883 asks of the install and initialize", "found": "source memory is all Phoenix memory, laid once per machine, kept up to date by a second install, and split into per-project blocks by initialize", "source": {"tier": "short-term", "path": "882/context/understand/20261025T102501Z/caps/cap-initialize/sources/issue-883.md", "start": 3, "end": 15, "fingerprint": "sha256:0dc31470aee14ed5ebb9569da91cc7c3dfd53feee295738c327007d7a3fc43cf", "text": "**All memory in Garura's source is Phoenix memory.** The memory written in Garura's source files is what gets deployed, so it is all Phoenix memory. One install lays the whole Phoenix memory on a machine, once. Then `initialize` sets up each project, and gives it only the memory blocks that fit it. A person installs Garura (Phoenix) once and initializes many projects, each with different memory blocks (the product owner, 2026-10-25).\n\n## Why\n\nToday the source memory and Phoenix memory drift apart. They are kept in step by hand. On 2026-10-25 the feature page template was in the source but in no memory tier, and Phoenix memory still had the old `functionality.md` (#868). A play that wrote a feature page could not be judged until the template was promoted by hand.\n\n## Done when\n\n- Every memory file in Garura's source is classed as Phoenix memory. No source memory file is \"project memory\" or sits outside Phoenix memory.\n- The install lays the whole Phoenix memory from the source on the machine, once. A second install brings it up to date. No hand promotion is needed.\n- `initialize` builds a project's Garura memory from Phoenix memory, with only the blocks its answers call for.\n- Two projects on one machine, initialized with different answers, get different memory blocks from the one Phoenix memory.\n- A play reads memory only through the memory tool, as now.", "version": null, "around": {"start": 1, "end": 18, "text": "# #883 All memory in Garura's source is Phoenix memory: one install lays it, initialize gives each project its own blocks\n\n**All memory in Garura's source is Phoenix memory.** The memory written in Garura's source files is what gets deployed, so it is all Phoenix memory. One install lays the whole Phoenix memory on a machine, once. Then `initialize` sets up each project, and gives it only the memory blocks that fit it. A person installs Garura (Phoenix) once and initializes many projects, each with different memory blocks (the product owner, 2026-10-25).\n\n## Why\n\nToday the source memory and Phoenix memory drift apart. They are kept in step by hand. On 2026-10-25 the feature page template was in the source but in no memory tier, and Phoenix memory still had the old `functionality.md` (#868). A play that wrote a feature page could not be judged until the template was promoted by hand.\n\n## Done when\n\n- Every memory file in Garura's source is classed as Phoenix memory. No source memory file is \"project memory\" or sits outside Phoenix memory.\n- The install lays the whole Phoenix memory from the source on the machine, once. A second install brings it up to date. No hand promotion is needed.\n- `initialize` builds a project's Garura memory from Phoenix memory, with only the blocks its answers call for.\n- Two projects on one machine, initialized with different answers, get different memory blocks from the one Phoenix memory.\n- A play reads memory only through the memory tool, as now.\n\n## Context\n"}}}], "from_update": null}
++{"citation_id": "cite-20261025-020", "issue": 882, "play": "discover-capabilities", "question": "Which knowledge shelf holds the capability that sets a project up for Garura after the install (cap-initialize)?", "answer": "None of the ten domain shelves; the capability is unmatched and grounded in the model domain page software-delivery-planning and the product owner sources #862, #881 and #883.", "decided_by": "product-os-keeper", "decided_at": "2026-10-25T11:25:01Z", "trace": [{"asked": "does the user-management shelf cover setting up a software project", "found": "its trigger is accounts, login, profiles, roles or access control in the product, not setting up a development project", "source": {"tier": "phoenix", "path": "knowledge/domains/user-management.md", "start": 5, "end": 5, "fingerprint": "sha256:0d1bf2f2e1209c7d6d5a1e838ccb147ec1f54c6983da2489dd32f7f25ad37d00", "text": "  trigger: \"the product needs accounts, login, profiles, roles, or access control\"", "version": "44e1c4c4799a", "around": {"start": 2, "end": 8, "text": "id: domains/user-management\ntitle: \"User Management: capabilities and when to include each (profile-driven)\"\nconditions:\n  trigger: \"the product needs accounts, login, profiles, roles, or access control\"\n  selection_keys: [shape.users, shape.surfaces, nfr.security, nfr.accessibility, compliance]\nprovenance: seeded\n---"}}}, {"asked": "does the collaboration shelf cover setting up a project tracker", "found": "its trigger is several people working on the same content, not a tracker set-up", "source": {"tier": "phoenix", "path": "knowledge/domains/collaboration.md", "start": 5, "end": 5, "fingerprint": "sha256:62918d2c5e6e55925a8d92e8a168a604d5be22e0b7a7b633b765adf67ac899e0", "text": "  trigger: \"more than one person works on the same content/object — comments, sharing, presence, co-editing\"", "version": "44e1c4c4799a", "around": {"start": 2, "end": 8, "text": "id: domains/collaboration\ntitle: \"Collaboration: people working together on shared things — real-time, presence, co-editing\"\nconditions:\n  trigger: \"more than one person works on the same content/object — comments, sharing, presence, co-editing\"\n  selection_keys: [real-time-need, co-edit-depth, sharing-permission-model, presence-need, scale]\nprovenance: Claude-drafted, reviewed and accepted (#434)\n---"}}}], "from_update": null}
++{"citation_id": "cite-20261025-021", "issue": 882, "play": "discover-capabilities", "question": "Which knowledge shelf does the capability Initialize a project for Garura (cap-initialize) sit on?", "answer": "None of the ten domain shelves; it is unmatched and grounded in the model's domain page software-delivery-planning and the product owner's sources #862, #881 and #883.", "decided_by": "product-os-keeper", "decided_at": "2026-10-25T12:35:34Z", "trace": [{"asked": "does a shelf trigger cover access control or roles for setting up a project", "found": "user-management's trigger is accounts, login, profiles, roles or access control for a product's users, not setting up a software project", "source": {"tier": "phoenix", "path": "knowledge/domains/user-management.md", "start": 5, "end": 5, "fingerprint": "sha256:0d1bf2f2e1209c7d6d5a1e838ccb147ec1f54c6983da2489dd32f7f25ad37d00", "text": "  trigger: \"the product needs accounts, login, profiles, roles, or access control\"", "version": "44e1c4c4799a", "around": {"start": 2, "end": 8, "text": "id: domains/user-management\ntitle: \"User Management: capabilities and when to include each (profile-driven)\"\nconditions:\n  trigger: \"the product needs accounts, login, profiles, roles, or access control\"\n  selection_keys: [shape.users, shape.surfaces, nfr.security, nfr.accessibility, compliance]\nprovenance: seeded\n---"}}}, {"asked": "does a shelf trigger cover a team working on shared work items", "found": "collaboration's trigger is people co-editing the same content, not a tracker's work-item types", "source": {"tier": "phoenix", "path": "knowledge/domains/collaboration.md", "start": 5, "end": 5, "fingerprint": "sha256:62918d2c5e6e55925a8d92e8a168a604d5be22e0b7a7b633b765adf67ac899e0", "text": "  trigger: \"more than one person works on the same content/object — comments, sharing, presence, co-editing\"", "version": "44e1c4c4799a", "around": {"start": 2, "end": 8, "text": "id: domains/collaboration\ntitle: \"Collaboration: people working together on shared things — real-time, presence, co-editing\"\nconditions:\n  trigger: \"more than one person works on the same content/object — comments, sharing, presence, co-editing\"\n  selection_keys: [real-time-need, co-edit-depth, sharing-permission-model, presence-need, scale]\nprovenance: Claude-drafted, reviewed and accepted (#434)\n---"}}}], "from_update": null}
++{"citation_id": "cite-20261025-022", "issue": 882, "play": "discover-capabilities", "question": "What does a memory block of initialize hold, and may initialize copy a Phoenix file into a project's Garura memory?", "answer": "A block is the relevant part of Phoenix memory an answer selects, down to the part of a file; initialize builds the project's memory from that part and never copies a Phoenix file. How the part is cut is our default.", "decided_by": "product-os-keeper", "decided_at": "2026-10-25T13:34:00Z", "trace": [{"asked": "what Garura memory is built from", "found": "only the part the project needs; the tool never copies a Phoenix file; checkout, not all of commerce", "source": {"tier": "short-term", "path": "882/context/understand/20261025T132647Z/caps/cap-initialize/sources/adr-039-decision-4.md", "start": 3, "end": 9, "fingerprint": "sha256:e7a292e59674243c1e3a2b475f165c22dd2a55b9f0f54863b90a83a0ec579634", "text": "### 4. The memory tool never copies a Phoenix file\n\n- The tool reads Phoenix memory. It does not copy a file from it into the project.\n- A project's Garura memory is built separately, from only the part the project needs: by `initialize`, and by promotion.\n- The tool's `pull`, and its copy on `read`, go.\n\nThe product owner: \"tool will not copy. it read the phoenix memory, and then it builds the project memry separately. For example, [Phoenix] has a memory about, let's say, a commerce domain, and let's say the project that we're working on only needs checkout in the commerce domain. You don't need to copy the entire file. You only need to bring in the context which is relevant to the checkout part of the commerce, so it cannot copy the file. It should not copy the file.\"", "version": null, "around": {"start": 1, "end": 12, "text": "# ADR 039, Decision 4 (committed on plan/788/base) and the glossary row for Garura memory\n\n### 4. The memory tool never copies a Phoenix file\n\n- The tool reads Phoenix memory. It does not copy a file from it into the project.\n- A project's Garura memory is built separately, from only the part the project needs: by `initialize`, and by promotion.\n- The tool's `pull`, and its copy on `read`, go.\n\nThe product owner: \"tool will not copy. it read the phoenix memory, and then it builds the project memry separately. For example, [Phoenix] has a memory about, let's say, a commerce domain, and let's say the project that we're working on only needs checkout in the commerce domain. You don't need to copy the entire file. You only need to bring in the context which is relevant to the checkout part of the commerce, so it cannot copy the file. It should not copy the file.\"\n\nGlossary (core/grounding/glossary.md, line 37):\n"}}}], "from_update": null}
++{"citation_id": "cite-20261025-023", "issue": 882, "play": "discover-capabilities", "question": "Are the six types initialize sets up all work-item types?", "answer": "No. They are the issue types Garura files; Business Intent is the one product-layer type among them, the other five are work items.", "decided_by": "product-os-keeper", "decided_at": "2026-10-25T13:34:07Z", "trace": [{"asked": "the layer of Business Intent in the tracker", "found": "Business Intent is a product-layer tracker issue labelled product, not a work item (ADR 048, as the review states it)", "source": {"tier": "short-term", "path": "882/context/understand/20261025T132647Z/caps/cap-initialize/prior/review-findings.yaml", "start": 38, "end": 38, "fingerprint": "sha256:bbec8eebfb95fe5875b658ebc08848bc682b6301096db0179c60d0ba615b52a7", "text": "    principle: \"The product model has a profile that is THE BOX (stage, users, surfaces, nfr, compliance, quality_vision). Two layers: the product layer (model) and the work layer (tracker issues: Epic, Story, Spike, Chore, Bug); Business Intent is a product-layer tracker issue labelled product, not a work item.\"", "version": null, "around": {"start": 35, "end": 41, "text": "      - \"origin/plan/788/base:core/components/plays/discover-capabilities/SKILL.md lines 462-530 (box-move decision), line 545 (decisions path)\"\n      - \"origin/plan/788/base:docs/adr/026-direct-to-model-writes.md lines 66-103 (direct write, keyed script writes shared files)\"\n  - id: G6\n    principle: \"The product model has a profile that is THE BOX (stage, users, surfaces, nfr, compliance, quality_vision). Two layers: the product layer (model) and the work layer (tracker issues: Epic, Story, Spike, Chore, Bug); Business Intent is a product-layer tracker issue labelled product, not a work item.\"\n    sources:\n      - \"origin/plan/788/base:core/components/memory/standards/schemas/product-os/spine.yaml lines 159-187\"\n      - \"origin/plan/788/base:docs/adr/048-the-product-model-and-the-work-are-two-layers.md lines 30-36\""}}}], "from_update": null}
