+++# Feature: Give each project its own memory blocks
+++
+++## What it does
+++- Words this page uses. Phoenix memory is the one shared memory on a machine (the domain shelves, the standards, the templates) that every project on that machine draws from. A project's Garura memory is the project's own memory, kept in the project's own folders and read before Phoenix memory. A memory block is the set of Phoenix memory files that one setup answer selects: for example the Java standards for a Java answer, the .NET standards for a .NET answer, the React front-end standards for a React answer, or one domain shelf. The block's selection is the place in Phoenix memory where the files for that choice are kept (for example the folder of the Java standards), so the block is every file found there. The brief is the summary `initialize` shows the product owner at the end of a run, where they type yes or no. What one block holds and how it is selected are our default; the sources say "blocks" and give the Java, .NET and React examples, but do not say what a block holds.
+++- Memory reaches a project in three steps, and this feature is the third. First, the Phoenix install runs once per machine and lays Phoenix memory from the Phoenix memory repository. Second, the Garura install runs once per project and lays only the pieces (the plays, agents, skills, tools and the memory tool) and no memory. Third, `initialize` runs in the project, asks the questions, and sets up the project's Garura memory from Phoenix memory with only the blocks the answers call for.
+++- It keeps each project's blocks its own. A person installs Phoenix once on a machine and initializes many projects there, each with different answers, so two projects hold different blocks taken from the one Phoenix memory, and initializing one project never changes another project or Phoenix memory.
+++- It records, in the project, which blocks the project took and what each held when it was taken, so the project can learn later that Phoenix memory changed. On each run, `initialize` compares the project's blocks with Phoenix memory and names in the brief each block that changed, so the product owner can take the change in. No install ever reaches into a project to do this.
+++
+++## Inputs / Outputs
+++- In: one project's confirmed setup answers (from Ask the setup questions, fn-setup-questions), which decide the blocks.
+++- In: Phoenix memory on the machine, read only through the memory tool. The tool's interface hides where Phoenix memory is kept: today it is a folder in the person's home folder that the Phoenix install laid, and later a remote place, and this feature does not change when that moves.
+++- In: on a later run, the project's own block list from the earlier run.
+++- Out: the project's own Garura memory, holding only the blocks its answers call for (the files are written through Build the project's memory, fn-project-memory).
+++- Out: the project's block list, kept in the project's own Garura memory beside the blocks. For each block it holds the answer that selected it, the block's selection, the place in Phoenix memory of each of its files, each file's fingerprint (a short code that changes when the file's content changes) and the version of Phoenix memory it was taken from. The selection is kept so that a later run can find a file Phoenix memory has added to the block; the fingerprints are kept so that it can find a file that changed. Where the list is kept and what it holds is our default; the sources do not say how a project records its blocks.
+++- Out: on a later run, a plain list in the brief of each block that changed in Phoenix memory since it was taken: the files changed, added or taken out, and the answer that selected the block.
+++- Out: when Phoenix memory cannot be reached, a plain message that says so and that the Phoenix install must run on this machine, and no memory built.
+++
+++## Rules & behavior
+++- The Garura install lays no memory. A project that has had the Garura install and no `initialize` run holds no Garura memory of its own, and `initialize` is the only part that sets it up.
+++- `initialize` reads Phoenix memory and writes only the project's own Garura memory. It never writes Phoenix memory and never writes another project's memory, because Phoenix memory is shared by every project on the machine and a write there from one project would change what the others draw from.
+++- A project takes a block only when one of its answers calls for it. Two projects with different answers (one Java, one .NET; one with a React front end, one with none) hold different blocks from the one Phoenix memory.
+++- How a project learns that a block changed. Each run of `initialize` reads through the memory tool, for each block in the list, the files its selection finds in Phoenix memory now and the current fingerprint of each. A file the list holds whose fingerprint differs is changed. A file the selection now finds that the list does not hold is added. A file the list holds that the selection no longer finds is taken out. The brief names each one under its block. Our default; the sources say only that `initialize` "can run again to take in changes".
+++- A changed block is taken in only on the product owner's typed yes in that run's brief, because the blocks a project holds are its product owner's choice and a new standard can change what a review finds. When the product owner says no, the project keeps the block as it was and the list keeps the old fingerprints, so the next run names the change again. Our default; the sources do not say who decides.
+++- What a yes is given to and what it does. The brief lists each changed, added or taken-out file under its block and asks for a yes or no to each file; a yes or no typed against a whole block applies to every file named under it. The reply is per file because one changed standard can be welcome and another in the same block not, and a yes for the whole block only would force the product owner to take both. A yes to a changed file overwrites the project's copy with the content Phoenix memory holds now and puts the new fingerprint in the block list. A yes to an added file copies it into the project's Garura memory and adds it, with its place in Phoenix memory and its fingerprint, to the block list. A yes to a taken-out file removes it from the project's Garura memory and from the block list, because a standard Phoenix memory no longer keeps is no longer kept up to date. A no to any of the three leaves that file and its block-list entry as they were, so the next run names it again, and each other file of the block follows its own reply. Our default; the sources do not say how a change is taken in.
+++- The check runs only when `initialize` runs. No install and no other play changes a project's memory or checks its blocks, so a project's memory never changes in the middle of other work. A product owner who wants a Phoenix memory change runs `initialize` again (Run initialize again, fn-rerun-initialize). Our default; the sources do not say when a project checks.
+++- A second Phoenix install or a second Garura install changes no file in any project's Garura memory. Phoenix memory may change under a project, but the project's own copy changes only on its next `initialize` run, after the product owner's yes.
+++- When the memory tool cannot reach Phoenix memory (it says the tier is missing), `initialize` stops, builds and changes no project memory, and says in plain words that Phoenix memory could not be reached and the Phoenix install must run on this machine. An empty answer there would look like "no block fits" and leave the project with too little memory. It never takes memory from Garura's source or from another project instead, because installed parts read memory only through the memory tool.
+++- A block whose answer the product owner changed is no longer a change to take in: the re-run adds or takes out the whole block from the new answer (Run initialize again, fn-rerun-initialize, owns that), because the new answer, not Phoenix memory, decided it.
+++
+++## Acceptance criteria
+++- Given a project after the Garura install and before `initialize`, when its Garura memory is read, then it holds no memory files.
+++- Given two projects on one machine, one answering Java and one answering .NET, when `initialize` runs in each, then only the first holds the Java standards block, only the second holds the .NET standards block, and both block lists name the same Phoenix memory as the source.
+++- Given a project already initialized, when `initialize` runs in a second project, then no file in the first project's Garura memory and no file in Phoenix memory changes.
+++- Given a project that took the database block, when a second Phoenix install changes a file in that block, then no file in the project's Garura memory changes.
+++- Given a project that took the database block and a file of that block changed in Phoenix memory, when `initialize` next runs in the project, then its brief names the database block and the changed file.
+++- Given a project that took the database block and Phoenix memory added a new file where the block's selection looks, when `initialize` next runs in the project, then its brief names the new file as added under the database block.
+++- Given a project that took the database block and Phoenix memory no longer keeps one of its files, when `initialize` next runs in the project, then its brief names that file as taken out under the database block.
+++- Given a brief that names an added file under the database block, when the product owner types yes to it, then the file is in the project's Garura memory and the block list holds it with its fingerprint.
+++- Given a brief that names a taken-out file under the database block, when the product owner types yes to it, then the file is no longer in the project's Garura memory or in the block list.
+++- Given a brief that names two changed files in the database block, when the product owner types yes to the first and no to the second, then the project holds the new content of the first and the old content of the second, and the block list holds the new fingerprint of the first and the old fingerprint of the second.
+++- Given a brief that names three changes under the database block, when the product owner types yes to the whole block, then all three are taken in.
+++- Given a brief that names a changed file in the database block, when the product owner types yes, then the project's database block holds the new content and the block list holds the new fingerprint.
+++- Given a brief that names a changed file in the database block, when the product owner says no, then the block and the block list are unchanged.
+++- Given a project whose product owner said no to a changed file on the last run, when `initialize` runs again, then the brief names the same change again.
+++- Given an initialized project, when Garura is installed again in it, then no file in that project's Garura memory changes.
+++- Given a machine where the memory tool cannot reach Phoenix memory, when `initialize` runs, then it builds no memory and says that the Phoenix install must run on this machine.
+++
+++## Out of scope
+++- Laying Phoenix memory on the machine and bringing it up to date: the Phoenix install owns it, once per machine. This feature only reads what that install laid.
+++- Laying the plays, agents, skills, tools and the memory tool in a project: the Garura install owns it, and it lays no memory.
+++- Which files each answer selects, writing them, and recording the answers in the product profile: Build the project's memory (fn-project-memory) owns it.
