+++{"citation_id": "cite-20261025-019", "issue": 882, "play": "/discover-capabilities", "question": "What does feature fn-883 of cap-initialize do?", "answer": "Every memory file in the source is Phoenix memory; one install lays it on the machine and a second install brings it up to date; initialize gives each project only the blocks its answers call for, so two projects on one machine get different blocks.", "decided_by": "product-os-keeper", "decided_at": "2026-10-25T10:40:20Z", "trace": [{"asked": "what #883 asks of the install and initialize", "found": "source memory is all Phoenix memory, laid once per machine, kept up to date by a second install, and split into per-project blocks by initialize", "source": {"tier": "short-term", "path": "882/context/understand/20261025T102501Z/caps/cap-initialize/sources/issue-883.md", "start": 3, "end": 15, "fingerprint": "sha256:0dc31470aee14ed5ebb9569da91cc7c3dfd53feee295738c327007d7a3fc43cf", "text": "**All memory in Garura's source is Phoenix memory.** The memory written in Garura's source files is what gets deployed, so it is all Phoenix memory. One install lays the whole Phoenix memory on a machine, once. Then `initialize` sets up each project, and gives it only the memory blocks that fit it. A person installs Garura (Phoenix) once and initializes many projects, each with different memory blocks (the product owner, 2026-10-25).\n\n## Why\n\nToday the source memory and Phoenix memory drift apart. They are kept in step by hand. On 2026-10-25 the feature page template was in the source but in no memory tier, and Phoenix memory still had the old `functionality.md` (#868). A play that wrote a feature page could not be judged until the template was promoted by hand.\n\n## Done when\n\n- Every memory file in Garura's source is classed as Phoenix memory. No source memory file is \"project memory\" or sits outside Phoenix memory.\n- The install lays the whole Phoenix memory from the source on the machine, once. A second install brings it up to date. No hand promotion is needed.\n- `initialize` builds a project's Garura memory from Phoenix memory, with only the blocks its answers call for.\n- Two projects on one machine, initialized with different answers, get different memory blocks from the one Phoenix memory.\n- A play reads memory only through the memory tool, as now.", "version": null, "around": {"start": 1, "end": 18, "text": "# #883 All memory in Garura's source is Phoenix memory: one install lays it, initialize gives each project its own blocks\n\n**All memory in Garura's source is Phoenix memory.** The memory written in Garura's source files is what gets deployed, so it is all Phoenix memory. One install lays the whole Phoenix memory on a machine, once. Then `initialize` sets up each project, and gives it only the memory blocks that fit it. A person installs Garura (Phoenix) once and initializes many projects, each with different memory blocks (the product owner, 2026-10-25).\n\n## Why\n\nToday the source memory and Phoenix memory drift apart. They are kept in step by hand. On 2026-10-25 the feature page template was in the source but in no memory tier, and Phoenix memory still had the old `functionality.md` (#868). A play that wrote a feature page could not be judged until the template was promoted by hand.\n\n## Done when\n\n- Every memory file in Garura's source is classed as Phoenix memory. No source memory file is \"project memory\" or sits outside Phoenix memory.\n- The install lays the whole Phoenix memory from the source on the machine, once. A second install brings it up to date. No hand promotion is needed.\n- `initialize` builds a project's Garura memory from Phoenix memory, with only the blocks its answers call for.\n- Two projects on one machine, initialized with different answers, get different memory blocks from the one Phoenix memory.\n- A play reads memory only through the memory tool, as now.\n\n## Context\n"}}}], "from_update": null}
